n achieving the bound.**  \n   Choose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,2025\\).  \n\n   *Lower‑triangle tiles:* for each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,2024\\) place the rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(r,c)\\mid k+1\\le r\\le 2025,\\;1\\le c\\le k\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which has size \\((2025-k)\\times k\\).  \n   These \\(2024\\) rectangles exactly cover all squares with column < row.\n\n   *Upper‑triangle tiles:* for each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,2024\\) place the rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k,\\;k+1\\le c\\le 2025\\},\n   \\]\n\n   of size \\(k\\times (2025-k)\\).  \n   These \\(2024\\) rectangles cover all squares with column > row.\n\n   The two families are disjoint, none of them contains a diagonal square, and together they cover every non‑diagonal unit square.  \n   Hence we have a tiling with exactly \\(2024+2024=4048\\) tiles.\n\nSince the lower bound equals the number of tiles in the explicit construction, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote by \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the permutation such that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is \\(\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); it contains exactly one element in each row and each column.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Geometry of a tile\n\nA rectangular tile is determined by a non‑empty interval of rows \\(R=[a,b]\\) and a non‑empty interval of columns \\(C=[c,d]\\); it covers all squares \\((r,c')\\) with \\(r\\in R,\\;c'\\in C\\).\n\nBecause no uncovered square may lie inside a tile, for every \\(i\\in R\\) we must have \\(\\pi(i)\\notin C\\).  \nConsequently one of the following holds:\n\n* **Below‑diagonal type:** every row in \\(R\\) is larger than every column in \\(C\\); i.e. \\(a> d\\).  \n  Then the tile lies entirely in the set \\(\\{(r,c)\\mid c<r\\}\\).\n\n* **Above‑diagonal type:** every row in \\(R\\) is smaller than every column in \\(C\\); i.e. \\(b< c\\).  \n  Then the tile lies entirely in the set \\(\\{(r,c)\\mid c>r\\}\\).\n\nNo tile can contain squares from both sides of the permutation, otherwise some pair \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) would be inside it.\n\nHence the board splits into two disjoint regions:\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(r,c)\\mid c<r\\},\\qquad U=\\{(r,c)\\mid c>r\\},\n\\]\n\nand every tile is wholly contained in either \\(L\\) or \\(U\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Lower bound for the number of tiles in \\(L\\)\n\nConsider a column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\).  \nAll squares of this column that belong to \\(L\\) are \\((i,j)\\) for \\(i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nLet a tile \\(T\\subseteq L\\) cover column \\(j\\).  \nWrite its row interval as \\(R=[a,b]\\) with \\(a\\le b\\).  \nSince \\(T\\subseteq L\\), we have \\(a>j\\) (otherwise a square \\((a,j)\\) would satisfy \\(j\\ge a\\), contradicting \\(c<r\\)).  \nThus the smallest row of \\(T\\) is some integer \\(a\\ge j+1\\).\n\nIf \\(a>j+1\\) then the squares \\((j+1,j),\\dots ,(a-1,j)\\) of column \\(j\\) are not covered by \\(T\\); they must be covered by another tile whose smallest row is at most \\(j+1\\).  \nConsequently **there must exist a tile whose smallest row equals exactly \\(j+1\\)** and which also covers column \\(j\\).\n\nFor different values of \\(j\\) the required smallest rows are distinct:\nthe tile needed for column \\(j\\) has smallest row \\(j+1\\).  \nHence we need at least one distinct tile for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\nTherefore any tiling of \\(L\\) requires **at least \\(n-1\\) tiles**.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the number of tiles in \\(U\\)\n\nThe same argument applied to the transpose of the board (swap rows and columns) shows that covering the upper‑triangle \\(U\\) also needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nSince the two families of tiles are disjoint, the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling satisfies  \n\