es**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nSince every tile lies wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThe uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles (covering \\(L\\)).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_c\\) is a \\( (n-c)\\times 1\\) tile; it occupies exactly the cells of column \\(c\\) that lie **below** the diagonal, i.e. the part of \\(L\\) in that column.\n\n*Horizontal tiles (covering \\(R\\)).*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid c=r+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_r\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-r)\\) tile; it occupies exactly the cells of row \\(r\\) that lie **to the right** of the diagonal, i.e. the part of \\(R\\) in that row.\n\nThe families \\(\\{V_c\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_r\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint because a cell either satisfies \\(c<r\\) (belongs to some \\(V_c\\)) or \\(c>r\\) (belongs to some \\(H_r\\)), never both.  \nTogether they cover every cell of the board except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{V_c\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{H_r\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Optimality\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction (2) achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------