nSince every tile belongs to either \\(L\\) or \\(R\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Vertical tiles (covering \\(L\\)).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_c\\) is a \\((n-c)\\times1\\) rectangle; all its cells satisfy \\(c<\\sigma(r)=r\\), so \\(V_c\\subseteq L\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles (covering \\(R\\)).*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid c=r+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_r\\) is a \\(1\\times(n-r)\\) rectangle; all its cells satisfy \\(c>\\sigma(r)=r\\), so \\(H_r\\subseteq R\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n- **Disjointness.**  \n  A cell \\((x,y)\\) with \\(y<c\\) cannot belong to any \\(H_r\\); a cell with \\(x>r\\) cannot belong to any \\(V_c\\).  \n  Moreover, for any cell either \\(y<x\\) (hence it lies in exactly one vertical tile \\(V_y\\)) or \\(y>x\\) (hence it lies in exactly one horizontal tile \\(H_x\\)).  \n  No cell belongs to both families, and no cell belongs to a tile that would cross the diagonal.\n\n- **Coverage.**  \n  If \\(y<x\\) then \\(y\\le x-1\\) and the vertical tile \\(V_y\\) contains all rows \\(r\\) with \\(r\\ge y+1\\); in particular it contains \\((x,y)\\).  \n  If \\(y>x\\) then the horizontal tile \\(H_x\\) contains all columns \\(c\\) with \\(c\\ge x+1\\); in particular it contains \\((x,y)\\).  \n  Hence every non‑diagonal cell of the board is covered exactly once.\n\n- **Uncovered squares.**  \n  The diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are not contained in any \\(V_c\\) (because \\(c=i\\) would require a row index \\(>i\\)) nor in any \\(H_r\\) (because \\(r=i\\) would require a column index \\(>i\\)).  \n  Thus precisely the diagonal cells remain uncovered, giving one uncovered cell in each row and each column.\n\n- **Number of tiles.**  \n  There are \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles \\(V_c\\) and \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles \\(H_r\\); total  \n\n  \\[\n  (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nInequality (7) shows that any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
