ncovered and a cell right of some (maybe different) uncovered but does not contain any uncovered? Let's try to find a counterexample. Suppose sigma = identity (i,i). Then L region is cells left of diagonal, R region is cells right of diagonal. Consider rectangle covering cells (1,2) (R) and (2,1) (L). That rectangle has rows 1..2, columns 1..2. It includes (1,1) and (2,2) which are uncovered squares. So fails. In identity case, any rectangle crossing the diagonal will contain some diagonal cell. But is that always true for any permutation? Let's try to find a rectangle that includes a left cell and a right cell but does not include any uncovered squares. Suppose sigma = (1->3,2->1,3->2) for n=3. Uncovered squares: (1,3), (2,1), (3,2). L region: row1: cols 1,2; row2: none; row3: col1. R region: row1: none; row2: cols 2,3; row3: col3. Consider rectangle covering (1,1) (L) and (2,3) (R). Rows 1..2, columns 1..3. Does it contain any uncovered squares? Uncovered squares: (1,3) is in row1 col3, which is inside rectangle. So fails. Consider rectangle covering (3,1) (L) and (2,2) (R). Rows 2..3, columns 1..2. Uncovered squares: (2,1) is row2 col1, inside rectangle. So fails. It seems plausible that any rectangle intersecting both L and R must contain at least one uncovered square. But is the proof given correct? Let's examine more thoroughly.\n\nGiven permutation sigma, define L = { (i,j) | j < sigma(i) }, R = { (i,j) | j > sigma(i) }. Suppose rectangle T covers a cell (i1,j1) in L, so j1 < sigma(i1). Also covers a cell (i2,j2) in R, so j2 > sigma(i2). Let rows interval be [a,b] containing i1,i2. Let columns interval be [c,d] containing j1,j2. Since j1 < sigma(i1) and j2 > sigma(i2), we have c ≤ j1 < sigma(i1) and j2 > sigma(i2) ≤ d. So sigma(i1) > j1 ≥ c, but sigma(i1) could be > d. However, we also have sigma(i2) < j2 ≤ d, but sigma(i2) could be < c. So we cannot guarantee sigma(i1) ≤ d or sigma(i2) ≥ c. But we can guarantee that sigma(i1) is > j1 and sigma(i2) is < j2. The rectangle's column interval includes all columns between c and d. Since sigma(i1) > j1, if sigma(i1) ≤ d, then sigma(i1) is inside. If sigma(i1) > d, then sigma(i1) is to the right of the rectangle. Similarly sigma(i2) could be left of rectangle. So it's possible that rectangle includes left cell from row i1 and right cell from row i2 but does not contain any uncovered squares.\n\nConsider a concrete example: n=5, sigma = identity (i,i). L region: cells left of diagonal. R region: cells right of diagonal. Suppose rectangle covers (i1=1,j1=1) (L) and (i2=5,j2=5) (R). Then rectangle rows 1..5, columns 1..5 includes all diagonal cells. So fails. But we need a rectangle that includes left cell from some row and right cell from some row but does not include any diagonal cell. Let's try to find a permutation where sigma(i1) is far right and sigma(i2) is far left, such that rectangle's column interval does not include either sigma(i1) or sigma(i2). For rectangle to include j1 < sigma(i1) and j2 > sigma(i2), we need j1 < sigma(i1) and j2 > sigma(i2). If sigma(i1) is far right, say sigma(i1) = n, and j1 = 1 (so j1 < n). If sigma(i2) is far left, say sigma(i2) = 1, and j2 = n (so j2 > 1). Then rectangle rows [i1,i2] includes both rows. Columns interval [1,n] includes all columns, so includes sigma(i1)=n and sigma(i2)=1. So includes uncovered squares. So that fails.\n\nBut maybe we can choose j1 and j2 such that rectangle's column interval does not include sigma(i1) or sigma(i2). For that, we need sigma(i1) > d (i.e., sigma(i1) > max(j1,j2)) and sigma(i2) < c (i.e., sigma(i2) < min(j1,j2)). But we also need j1 < sigma(i1) and j2 > sigma(i2). So we need j1 < sigma(i1) > d, so sigma(i1) > d >= j2. So sigma(i1) > j2. Similarly sigma(i2) < c <= j1. So sigma(i2) < j1. So we need sigma(i1) > j2 and sigma(i2) < j1. So sigma(i1) > j2 > sigma(i2). So sigma(i1) > sigma(i2). That's fine. So we need a permutation sigma where there exist rows i1,i2 such that sigma(i1) > sigma(i2). That's always possible unless sigma is monotonic increasing (identity). But we need also j1 < sigma(i1) and j2 > sigma(i2). Choose j1 = sigma(i2) (or less) and j2 = sigma(i1) (or greater). But we need j1 < sigma(i1) (true if j1 <= sigma(i2) < sigma(i1)). And j2 > sigma(i2) (true if j2 >= sigma(i1) > sigma(i2)). So we can set j1 = sigma(i2) and j2 = sigma(i1). Then rectangle columns interval [sigma(i2), sigma(i1)] includes sigma(i2) and sigma(i1). Actually includes both sigma(i2) and sigma(i1) because they are endpoints. So rectangle includes uncovered squares. So that fails.\n\nBut we could try to set j1 < sigma(i2) maybe? Wait j1 must be < sigma(i1). If sigma(i2) < sigma(i1), we can set j1 = sigma(i2) - 1 (if sigma(i2) > 1). Then j1 < sigma(i1) (since sigma(i2)-1 < sigma(i2) < sigma(i1)). And j2 > sigma(i2). Choose j2 = sigma(i2) + 1 maybe? But we need j2 > sigma(i2). Also j2 must be > sigma(i2). Choose j2 = sigma(i2) + 1. Then rectangle columns interval [j1,j2] = [sigma(i2)-1, sigma(i2)+1]. Does this include sigma(i1)? Possibly not if sigma(i1) > sigma(i2)+1. So sigma(i1) > d. So sigma(i1) not in rectangle. Does it include sigma(i2)? sigma(i2) is between j1 and j2, so sigma(i2) is inside rectangle. So rectangle includes uncovered square (i2, sigma(i2)). So fails.\n\nThus any rectangle that includes a right cell from row i2 must have column interval that includes sigma(i2) if sigma(i2) lies between j1 and j2. But we could try to have sigma(i2) < j1 < j2 < sigma(i1). Then rectangle columns interval [j1,j2] does not include sigma(i2) (since sigma(i2) < j1) nor sigma(i1) (since sigma(i1) > j2). But we need j1 < sigma(i1) (true) and j2 > sigma(i2) (true). So we can pick j1 and j2 such that sigma(i2) < j1 < j2 < sigma(i1). Then rectangle columns interval does not contain either sigma(i2) or sigma(i1). However, we also need j1 < sigma(i1) (true) and j2 > sigma(i2) (true). So rectangle includes a left cell (i1,j1) with j1 < sigma(i1) and a right cell (i2,j2) with j2 > sigma(i2). But the rectangle's column interval does not include sigma(i1) or sigma(i2). So the rectangle may avoid uncovered squares. Let's test with a concrete permutation.\n\nTake n=5. Let sigma = (1->5, 2->1, 3->2, 4->3, 5->4). So sigma(1)=5, sigma(2)=1, sigma(3)=2, sigma(4)=3, sigma(5)=4. Uncovered squares: (1,5), (2,1), (3,2), (4,3), (5,4). Let's pick i1=1 (sigma(1)=5). Choose j1=3 (j1 < sigma(1)=5). i2=2 (sigma(2)=1). Choose j2=2 (j2 > sigma(2)=1). Then rectangle rows interval [1,2], columns interval [2,3] (since min(j1,j2)=2, max=3). Does rectangle contain any uncovered squares? Uncovered squares in rows 1 or 2: (1,5) column 5 not in [2,3]; (2,1) column 1 not in [2,3]. So rectangle does NOT contain any uncovered squares. It contains cells (1,3) (L) and (2,2) (R). Indeed (2,2) is right of sigma(2)=1. So rectangle intersects both L and R but avoids uncovered squares. So the claim \"No tile may intersect both L and R\" is false. Indeed we have found a rectangle that includes a left cell from row1 and a right cell from row2 but does not contain any uncovered squares. However, the rectangle includes cells (1,3) and (2,2). Are these cells allowed to be covered by a tile? Yes, they are in L and R respectively. So a tile could be placed covering those cells. But the solution's argument says it's impossible. So the lower bound argument may be flawed.\n\nBut we need to consider the condition that each unit square is covered by at most one tile. That's fine. The tile can be any rectangle covering any set of cells as long as they are not uncovered squares. So the rectangle we found is a valid tile under the problem constraints. So the claim that each tile must be wholly in L or wholly in R is false. Therefore the lower bound that each region needs at least n-1 tiles may not hold. However, the solution's lower bound uses the fact that each region (L and R) must be tiled separately. If a tile can cross from L to R without covering uncovered squares, then the lower bound may be lower.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound might be too high. The minimal number of tiles might be less than 2n-2.\n\nWe need to verify the actual minimal number. Let's think about the problem more generally.\n\nWe have a permutation matrix of uncovered squares. We want to tile the rest of the board with axis-aligned rectangles, each covering disjoint cells, covering all cells except the uncovered squares. The goal is to minimize number of rectangles (tiles). This is essentially a covering of the complement of a permutation matrix by axis-aligned rectangles (disjoint). Equivalent to partitioning the board into rectangles that avoid the permutation points.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the board minus a permutation matrix.\n\nThis is reminiscent of a known combinatorial problem: For an n×n board with one hole per row and column, the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover the rest is something like n? Or maybe 2n-2? Let's think.\n\nConsider the identity permutation (holes on diagonal). Then the board minus diagonal can be partitioned into 2n-2 rectangles: each row i has a left segment (columns 1..i-1) and right segment (columns i+1..n). Those are horizontal strips? Actually each row i has two intervals: left of diagonal (i-1 cells) and right of diagonal (n-i cells). We could tile each left segment as a vertical rectangle covering column j across rows > j? Wait we need rectangles that can be larger than a single row. For identity, we can tile the left region by vertical strips: for each column j (1 ≤ j ≤ n-1), take rectangle covering rows j+1..n, column j (a vertical rectangle of height n-j). That covers all cells left of diagonal. Similarly, tile right region by horizontal strips: for each row i (1 ≤ i ≤ n-1), take rectangle covering row i, columns i+1..n (horizontal rectangle of width n-i). This yields (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 rectangles. That's the construction given.\n\nBut could we do better? Perhaps we can combine some left and right cells into a larger rectangle that goes around a hole. For identity, any rectangle that includes both left and right cells would cross the diagonal and thus include a diagonal cell (hole). However, as we saw earlier, for identity permutation, any rectangle crossing the diagonal must include a diagonal cell because the diagonal is monotone: For any rows i1 < i2, the diagonal positions are (i1,i1) and (i2,i2). If a rectangle includes a left cell (i1,j1) with j1 < i1 and a right cell (i2,j2) with j2 > i2, then the rectangle's column interval is [j1,j2] which includes i1 and i2? Not necessarily. Let's examine identity case: Suppose we pick left cell (i1,j1) with j1 < i1. Right cell (i2,j2) with j2 > i2. For rectangle to avoid diagonal cells, we need that for each row k in [i1,i2], the column interval [j1,j2] does not contain k. But if j1 < i1 and j2 > i2, then for any k between i1 and i2, we have j1 < i1 ≤ k ≤ i2 < j2, so k ∈ [j1,j2]. So diagonal cell (k,k) lies inside rectangle. So identity case indeed any rectangle crossing both sides will contain a diagonal cell. So for identity permutation, the claim holds.\n\nBut for a non-identity permutation, there might be a rectangle crossing both sides without covering any uncovered squares. The solution's lower bound uses the fact that the multiset of left lengths ℓ_i = sigma(i)-1 is a permutation of {0,...,n-1} (since sigma is a permutation). That holds for any permutation. They argue that each distinct positive ℓ_i requires a rectangle with rightmost column equal to that ℓ_i. But this argument may be flawed because a rectangle covering left region may have rightmost column equal to some ℓ_i but also could cover multiple rows with same ℓ_i? Actually they argue each distinct positive ℓ_i requires at least one rectangle. However, they didn't consider that a rectangle could have rightmost column equal to ℓ_i and cover multiple rows with that ℓ_i? That is okay; they only need at least one rectangle per distinct ℓ_i. Since ℓ_i values are all distinct (except zero), there are n-1 distinct positive ℓ_i, so at least n-1 rectangles needed for L. But is it true that each distinct ℓ_i requires a distinct rectangle? Let's examine.\n\nDefine ℓ_i = sigma(i)-1, the number of cells left of uncovered square in row i. Consider a rectangle Q ⊆ L. Its rightmost column c(Q). For any row i that Q covers, we must have ℓ_i ≥ c(Q). The rectangle may cover rows with ℓ_i > c(Q) as well. The rectangle's rightmost column is c(Q). The argument says: For each distinct positive ℓ_i, there must be at least one rectangle whose rightmost column equals that ℓ_i. They argue that for each row i with ℓ_i > 0, the cell (i, ℓ_i) is the rightmost cell of L in that row. Any rectangle covering this cell must have rightmost column ℓ_i. But it's possible that a rectangle covering that cell also covers other rows with ℓ_j > ℓ_i, but its rightmost column is ℓ_i. So each ℓ_i appears as rightmost column of some rectangle covering that row's rightmost cell. However, could the same rectangle cover the rightmost cells of multiple rows with different ℓ_i? No, because if rectangle's rightmost column is ℓ_i, then any row j it covers must have ℓ_j ≥ ℓ_i. If ℓ_j > ℓ_i, then the rightmost cell (j, ℓ_j) is not covered because its column ℓ_j > ℓ_i, which is beyond rectangle's rightmost column. So rectangle cannot cover rightmost cells of rows with larger ℓ_j. So each distinct ℓ_i (positive) must be the rightmost column of some rectangle covering that row's rightmost cell. However, could multiple rows share the same ℓ_i? In a permutation, ℓ_i values are distinct because sigma(i) is a permutation, thus sigma(i)-1 yields a permutation of {0,...,n-1}. So each positive ℓ_i is distinct. So indeed we need at least n-1 rectangles to cover all left cells. But is it possible to use fewer rectangles by covering some rows' left cells with rectangles that have rightmost column less than ℓ_i? That would not cover the rightmost cell (i, ℓ_i) of that row, leaving it uncovered. But each left cell must be covered. So we need to cover the rightmost left cell of each row with ℓ_i>0. That requires a rectangle whose rightmost column is ℓ_i. So indeed at least n-1 rectangles needed for L.\n\nBut is it possible to have a rectangle that covers left cells of rows i and j where ℓ_i < ℓ_j, but rectangle's rightmost column is ℓ_i, covering row i's rightmost left cell, and also covering some left cells of row j (which are columns ≤ ℓ_i). Row j's rightmost left cell (j, ℓ_j) would not be covered, but we could cover it with another rectangle with rightmost column ℓ_j. So each ℓ_i requires its own rectangle. So lower bound for L is n-1. That seems correct.\n\nNow the earlier claim that no tile can intersect both L and R is not needed for this lower bound; we can treat L and R separately because any tile that intersects both would still be counted as either left or right? Actually if a tile intersects both L and R, it would be counted as covering some left cells and some right cells. The lower bound argument for L considered only tiles wholly in L. If there are tiles that also cover right cells, they could also be used to cover left cells, potentially reducing the number of left-only tiles needed. But the lower bound argument for L assumed that each tile covering left cells is wholly in L. If there is a tile that covers left cells and also some right cells, it could be used to cover left cells, but then it would also be counted as a tile covering right cells. That might reduce the total number of tiles needed overall, but the lower bound for L alone might not hold because some left cells may be covered by tiles that also cover right cells. However, the argument that each distinct ℓ_i requires a rectangle with rightmost column ℓ_i does not require that rectangle be wholly in L; it could be a rectangle that also extends into R as long as it does not cover uncovered squares. But can a rectangle that extends into R have rightmost column ℓ_i? That would mean its rightmost column is ℓ_i, so it cannot extend beyond ℓ_i to the right, so it cannot include any right cells (which have column > sigma(i) >= ℓ_i+1). However, a rectangle that includes left cells from some rows and right cells from other rows could have rightmost column ℓ_i for some row i, but for rows where it includes right cells, those rows must have sigma(row) < ℓ_i? Actually right cells are those with column > sigma(row). If rectangle's rightmost column is ℓ_i, then any right cell it contains must have column > sigma(row) but ≤ ℓ_i. That would require sigma(row) < ℓ_i. But right cells are defined as columns > sigma(row). So if sigma(row) < ℓ_i, then some columns between sigma(row)+1 and ℓ_i are right cells for that row. So rectangle could include right cells of rows where sigma(row) < ℓ_i. But those rows also have left cells up to sigma(row)-1. So rectangle could include both left and right cells for different rows. However, does it intersect both L and R? Yes, it would contain left cells for rows where sigma(row) > ℓ_i? Actually left cells are columns < sigma(row). If rectangle's rightmost column is ℓ_i, then for rows where sigma(row) > ℓ_i, the rectangle includes columns up to ℓ_i, which are left cells for those rows. For rows where sigma(row) < ℓ_i, the rectangle includes columns up to ℓ_i, which includes some right cells (columns > sigma(row)). So rectangle can intersect both L and R, but its rightmost column is ℓ_i. So the lower bound argument for L still holds: each ℓ_i > 0 must be the rightmost column of some rectangle covering the cell (i, ℓ_i). That rectangle may also cover some right cells of other rows, but that does not reduce the number of distinct rightmost columns needed: we still need a rectangle for each ℓ_i. However, could a single rectangle have rightmost column ℓ_i and also cover the rightmost left cell of another row j with ℓ_j = ℓ_i? No, ℓ_j distinct. So each ℓ_i requires a rectangle with rightmost column ℓ_i. So at least n-1 rectangles needed overall (including those that may also cover right cells). Similarly for right region, each distinct r_i > 0 requires a rectangle with leftmost column sigma(i)+1 (i.e., leftmost column = sigma(i)+1). But a rectangle that covers left cells may also serve as a rectangle for right region? No, because leftmost column for right region is sigma(i)+1, which is > sigma(i). A rectangle that covers left cells has rightmost column ≤ ℓ_i = sigma(i)-1, so cannot have leftmost column > sigma(i). So a rectangle cannot serve both a left region requirement and a right region requirement simultaneously. However, a rectangle that intersects both L and R could have leftmost column > sigma(i) for some rows and rightmost column < sigma(i) for other rows. But the leftmost column of that rectangle is at least some value, maybe > sigma(i) for some rows. But the rectangle's leftmost column is the minimum column among all cells it covers. If it covers left cells, those columns are < sigma(row). So leftmost column is less than sigma(row) for those rows. So leftmost column cannot be > sigma(i) for all rows. So the rectangle cannot serve as a rectangle with leftmost column equal to sigma(i)+1 for any row, because its leftmost column is lower. So a rectangle cannot satisfy the requirement for right region (leftmost column = sigma(i)+1) if it also contains left cells. So the left region rectangles and right region rectangles are distinct sets.\n\nThus the lower bound of at least (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 rectangles holds regardless of whether some rectangles intersect both L and R. However, we need to verify that the argument that each distinct ℓ_i requires a rectangle is sound even if rectangles can intersect both L and R. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have ℓ_i = sigma(i)-1. For each row i with ℓ_i > 0, the cell (i, ℓ_i) is in L (the rightmost left cell). Any rectangle covering this cell must have rightmost column at least ℓ_i (since it's the rightmost column of rectangle). Actually the rectangle's rightmost column must be >= ℓ_i. But if the rectangle's rightmost column > ℓ_i, then it would include column ℓ_i+1 in that row i, which is either uncovered square (if ℓ_i+1 = sigma(i))? Wait sigma(i) = ℓ_i+1. So column ℓ_i+1 is the uncovered square (i, sigma(i)). So if rectangle's rightmost column > ℓ_i, then rectangle would include column sigma(i) in row i, covering the uncovered square, which is not allowed. So rectangle covering (i, ℓ_i) must have rightmost column exactly ℓ_i. So any rectangle covering that cell must have rightmost column ℓ_i. This is true regardless of whether rectangle also covers other rows. So each ℓ_i > 0 corresponds to a rectangle with rightmost column ℓ_i. However, could the same rectangle cover multiple rightmost left cells for rows i and j with ℓ_i = ℓ_j? Not possible because ℓ_i distinct. So each ℓ_i > 0 corresponds to a distinct rectangle (or at least distinct rightmost column). But could a rectangle covering (i, ℓ_i) also cover (j, ℓ_j) where ℓ_j < ℓ_i? That would require rectangle's rightmost column ℓ_i, which is > ℓ_j, so column ℓ_j is within rectangle, so (j, ℓ_j) is covered. That is okay. So a rectangle with rightmost column ℓ_i could cover rightmost left cells of rows with ℓ_j ≤ ℓ_i. But ℓ_i are distinct. So the rectangle with rightmost column ℓ_i may also cover rightmost left cells for rows with smaller ℓ_j. However, those rows' rightmost left cells have ℓ_j < ℓ_i, so they are not the rightmost left cell of those rows? Actually they are the rightmost left cell of those rows, because ℓ_j is the rightmost left column for row j. If rectangle's rightmost column is ℓ_i > ℓ_j, then the rectangle includes column ℓ_j, which is the rightmost left cell of row j. So rectangle covers that cell as well. So a rectangle with rightmost column ℓ_i can cover multiple rows' rightmost left cells, as long as those rows have ℓ_j ≤ ℓ_i. But then the rectangle's rightmost column is ℓ_i, which is the maximum ℓ among rows it 