}(k)=\\#\\{\\text{contiguous blocks of rows }i\\text{ with }p(i)>k\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Analogously, for the right region \\(R\\) (with heights \\(r_i=n-p(i)\\)) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   T_R(p)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} C_{\\le}(k),\\qquad \n   C_{\\le}(k)=\\#\\{\\text{contiguous blocks of rows }i\\text{ with }p(i)\\le n-k\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Universal lower bound.**  \n   For every \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the sets \\(\\{i:p(i)>k\\}\\) and \\(\\{i:p(i)\\le n-k\\}\\) are non‑empty (the maximum of \\(p\\) is \\(n\\) and the minimum is \\(1\\)).  Consequently each of the numbers \\(C_{>}(k)\\) and \\(C_{\\le}(k)\\) is at least \\(1\\).  Summing (2) and (3) gives the general lower bound\n   \\[\n   T(p)=T_L(p)+T_R(p)\\ge\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}(1+1)=2(n-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n   - For the left region \\(L\\) we have \\(\\{i:p(i)>k\\}=\\{k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a single contiguous block; hence \\(C_{>}(k)=1\\) for every \\(k\\).  \n   - For the right region \\(R\\) we have \\(\\{i:p(i)\\le n-k\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), also a single block, so \\(C_{\\le}(k)=1\\) for every \\(k\\).  \n   Therefore \\(T(p)=2(n-1)\\).\n\n   An explicit tiling uses:\n   * **Vertical tiles:** for each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) take the rectangle consisting of all squares in column \\(j\\) below the diagonal (rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\)).  This gives \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n   * **Horizontal tiles:** for each row \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) take the rectangle consisting of all squares in row \\(i\\) to the right of the diagonal (columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\)).  This gives another \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n   The two families are disjoint, cover every square except the diagonal holes, and use exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   By (4) no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum number of tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The board consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with\n\\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  A tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; a tile therefore corresponds to a set\n\\[\nI\\times J=\\{(i,j):i\\in I,\\;j\\in J\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are non‑empty intervals of consecutive integers.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there is a function\n\\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\nThe column condition forces \\(p\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(p\\) is a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Any tile lies completely in the left or in the right region  \n\nDefine\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad R=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nIf a rectangle \\(I\\times J\\) contained a point of both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\), then there would exist\n\\(i\\in I\\) with \\(p(i)\\in J\\); the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) would belong to the rectangle,\ncontradicting the condition that uncovered squares are not covered.\nConsequently each tile is either a subset of \\(L\\) (a *left‑tile*) or a subset of \\(R\\) (a *right‑tile*).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Histogram model  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) let  \n\\[\nh_i=p(i)-1\\qquad\\text{(length of the left segment in row }i),\n\\]\n\\[\nr_i=n-p(i)\\qquad\\text{(length of the right segment in row }i).\n\\]\nThe set \\(L\\) is exactly the histogram formed by the column‑wise heights \\((h_i)_{i=1}^n\\);\nthe set \\(R\\) is the histogram formed by \\((r_i)_{i=1}^n\\).\n\nThus the problem of tiling \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)) by rectangles is identical to partitioning the\nhistogram with heights \\(h_i\\) (resp. \\(r_i\\)) into axis‑parallel rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma (minimum number of rectangles for a histogram)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(h_1,\\dots ,h_n\\) be non‑negative integers.  For each integer \\(k\\ge1\\) define\n\\[\nC(k)=\\#\\{\\text{maximal contiguous blocks of rows }i\\text{ with }h_i\\ge k\\}.\n\\]\nThen the smallest possible number of rectangles whose union is exactly the set\n\\(\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le h_i\\}\\) equals\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\ge1}C(k).\n\\tag{L}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider scanning the rows from top (\\(i=1\\)) to bottom (\\(i=n\\)).  \nMaintain a stack of active heights.  When we encounter a row with height \\(h_i\\):\n* while the stack top exceeds \\(h_i\\) we pop the top; each pop closes a rectangle\n  whose height equals the popped value and whose vertical span is the interval\n  from the row where that height was pushed up to the current row minus one;\n* if the stack top is smaller than \\(h_i\\), we push \\(h_i\\) onto the stack,\n  thereby opening a new rectangle that will later be closed when a lower height\n  appears.\n\nEvery push creates exactly one rectangle, and every rectangle created in this\nway is necessary: a rectangle of height \\(k\\) can cover only rows belonging to a\nsingle contiguous block of the set \\(\\{i:h_i\\ge k\\}\\); different blocks of the same\nheight cannot be merged because a rectangle would have to contain a row where\n\\(h_i<k\\).  Hence the number of pushes equals the total number of blocks over\nall heights, which is precisely the right‑hand side of (L).  No tiling can use\nfewer rectangles, because each block of height \\(k\\) must be covered by a\ndifferent rectangle. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for any permutation  \n\nApply the lemma to the left histogram \\((h_i)\\).  For each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the\nset \\(\\{i:h_i\\ge k\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{i:p(i)>k\\}\\).  Denote\n\\[\nC_{>}(k)=\\#\\{\\text{contiguous blocks of rows }i\\text{ with }p(i)>k\\}.\n\\]\nSimilarly, for the right histogram we obtain\n\\[\nC_{\\le}(k)=\\#\\{\\text{contiguous blocks of rows }i\\text{ with }p(i)\\le n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently any tiling of the whole board uses at least\n\\[\nT(p)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\bigl(C_{>}(k)+C_{\\le}(k)\\bigr)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\nFor a fixed \\(k\\) the set \\(\\{i:p(i)>k\\}\\) is non‑empty because the maximum of the\npermutation is \\(n>k\\); likewise \\(\\{i:p(i)\\le n-k\\}\\) is non‑empty because the minimum\nis \\(1\\le n-k\\).  Each non‑empty set contains at least one contiguous block, so\n\\(C_{>}(k)\\ge1\\) and \\(C_{\\le}(k)\\ge1\\).  Summation of (5) yields the universal lower\nbound\n\\[\nT(p)\\ge\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}(1+1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor any \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we have\n\\[\n\\{i:p(i)>k\\}=\\{k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\n\\{i:p(i)\\le n-k\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\},\n\\]\nboth of which are single contiguous blocks.  Hence\n\\(C_{>}(k)=C_{\\le}(k)=1\\) for every \\(k\\), and (5) gives\n\\(T(p)=2(n-1)\\).\n\nAn explicit tiling uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles:\n\n*For the left side.*  For each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) take the vertical rectangle\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhich consists of all squares in column \\(j\\) below the diagonal.  These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and cover the whole left region.\n\n*For the right side.*  For each row \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) take the horizontal rectangle\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhich consists of all squares in row \\(i\\