r any permutation  \n\nApply the lemma to the left histogram \\((h_i)\\).  For each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the\nset \\(\\{i:h_i\\ge k\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{i:p(i)>k\\}\\).  Denote\n\\[\nC_{>}(k)=\\#\\{\\text{contiguous blocks of rows }i\\text{ with }p(i)>k\\}.\n\\]\nSimilarly, for the right histogram we obtain\n\\[\nC_{\\le}(k)=\\#\\{\\text{contiguous blocks of rows }i\\text{ with }p(i)\\le n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently any tiling of the whole board uses at least\n\\[\nT(p)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\bigl(C_{>}(k)+C_{\\le}(k)\\bigr)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\nFor a fixed \\(k\\) the set \\(\\{i:p(i)>k\\}\\) is non‑empty because the maximum of the\npermutation is \\(n>k\\); likewise \\(\\{i:p(i)\\le n-k\\}\\) is non‑empty because the minimum\nis \\(1\\le n-k\\).  Each non‑empty set contains at least one contiguous block, so\n\\(C_{>}(k)\\ge1\\) and \\(C_{\\le}(k)\\ge1\\).  Summation of (5) yields the universal lower\nbound\n\\[\nT(p)\\ge\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}(1+1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor any \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we have\n\\[\n\\{i:p(i)>k\\}=\\{k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\n\\{i:p(i)\\le n-k\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\},\n\\]\nboth of which are single contiguous blocks.  Hence\n\\(C_{>}(k)=C_{\\le}(k)=1\\) for every \\(k\\), and (5) gives\n\\(T(p)=2(n-1)\\).\n\nAn explicit tiling uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles:\n\n*For the left side.*  For each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) take the vertical rectangle\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhich consists of all squares in column \\(j\\) below the diagonal.  These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and cover the whole left region.\n\n*For the right side.*  For each row \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) take the horizontal rectangle\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhich consists of all squares in row \\(i\\) to