\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Denote \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares as a permutation\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square,\nthe set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a\nbijection \\(\\pi:[n]\\to[n]\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(\\pi\\) define, for every row \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j):\\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe collection of all covered squares is the disjoint union\n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Every tile lies completely in \\(L\\) or completely in \\(R\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\quad\n\\text{If a rectangle contains squares from both }L_i\\text{ and }R_i\\text{ for some }i,\n\\text{ then it also contains }(i,\\pi(i)).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle \\(T\\) contains \\((i,j_1)\\in L_i\\) and\n\\((i,j_2)\\in R_i\\).  Then \\(j_1<\\pi(i)<j_2\\).  Because \\(T\\) is axis‑aligned,\nit contains every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j_1\\le j\\le j_2\\); in particular it\ncontains \\((i,\\pi(i))\\), which must stay uncovered – a contradiction. ∎\n\nHence each tile is either a **left‑tile** (contained in \\(L\\)) or a **right‑tile**\n(contained in \\(R\\)).  Let \\(t_L\\) and \\(t_R\\) be the numbers of left‑ and\nright‑tiles, respectively, and \\(t=t_L+t_R\\) the total number of tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction using \\(2n-2\\) tiles (upper bound)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place\n\n* a horizontal tile covering the whole left interval \\(L_i\\) if \\(\\pi(i)>1\\);\n* a horizontal tile covering the whole right interval \\(R_i\\) if \\(\\pi(i)<n\\).\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows.\nExactly one row satisfies \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) (no left interval) and exactly one row\nsatisfies \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) (no right interval).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nt_L=n-1,\\qquad t_R=n-1,\\qquad t=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(2n-2\\) tiles always suffice**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Rank of the left and right region matrices\n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) matrices\n\n\\[\nL_{i,j}= \\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }j<\\pi(i),\\\\[2pt]\n0 &\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nR_{i,j}= \\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }j>\\pi(i),\\\\[2pt]\n0 &\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\quad \\operatorname{rank}(L)=\\operatorname{rank}(R)=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\(P\\) be the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\), i.e.\n\\(P_{i,\\pi(i)}=1\\).  Consider the similarity transformation\n\\(P\\,L\\,P^{\\mathsf T}\\).  Its \\((i,j)\\)-entry equals\n\n\\[\n(P\\,L\\,P^{\\mathsf T})_{i,j}=L_{\\pi^{-1}(i),\\pi^{-1}(j)}\n=1\\iff \\pi^{-1}(j)<\\pi^{-1}(i)\\iff j<i .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P\\,L\\,P^{\\mathsf T}\\) is the strictly lower‑triangular matrix\n\\(J_{<}\\) that has a 1 in every position below the main diagonal and 0 elsewhere.\n\\(J_{<}\\) has rank \\(n-1\\) (its first row is zero, the remaining \\(n-1\\) rows are\nlinearly independent).  Rank is invariant under permutation similarity, so\n\\(\\operatorname{rank}(L)=n-1\\).\n\nThe matrix \\(R\\) is the transpose of \\(L\\) after a column permutation, therefore\n\\(\\operatorname{rank}(R)=n-1\\) as well. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEach left‑tile \\(T\\) is a rectangle \\(R_T\\times C_T\\) with\n\\(R_T\\subseteq[n]\\), \\(C_T\\subseteq[n]\\).  Its incidence matrix is the outer\nproduct \\(u_T v_T^{\\mathsf T}\\), where \\(u_T\\) (resp. \\(v_T\\)) is the\n\\(0\\!-\\!1\\) characteristic vector of \\(R_T\\) (resp. \\(C_T\\)).\nSumming over all left‑tiles gives\n\n\\[\nL=\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal L} u_T v_T^{\\mathsf T}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(\\operatorname{rank}(L)\\le |\\mathcal L|=t_L\\).  By Lemma 2,\n\\(\\operatorname{rank}(L)=n-1\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nt_L\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same argument applied to the right region yields\n\n\\[\nt_R\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdding (1) and (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nt=t_L+t_R\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe construction of Section 3 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower bound of\nSection 5 shows that fewer tiles are impossible.  Hence the minimum possible\nnumber of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Self‑Correction Instruction ###\n\n- Verify that the permutation model correctly captures the “exactly one uncovered\n  square per row and column”.\n- Ensure Lemma 1’s argument about a rectangle crossing a hole is explicit and\n  covers all possibilities.\n- Confirm that the rank computation in Lemma 2 indeed yields a strictly\n  lower‑triangular matrix after the similarity transformation.\n- Check that the rank argument for the lower bound correctly treats left‑ and\n  right‑tiles separately and that the inequality \\(t\\ge2n-2\\) follows.\n- Re‑read the construction to confirm that the tile count is exactly\n  \\(2n-2\\) (one row lacks a left tile, one row lacks a right tile).  \nAll steps are present, logically ordered, and use only the stated lemmas."
