\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n\n#### 1.  Matrix formulation  \n\nDefine the binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{ij})_{1\\le i,j\\le n},\\qquad \na_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if the unit square in row }i\\text{ and column }j\\text{ is covered},\\\\[4pt]\n0 &\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of zeros of \\(A\\) consists of exactly one entry in each row and each column. Hence the zero pattern is a permutation matrix; there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\na_{i,\\sigma(i)}=0\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n),\n\\]\nand all other entries of \\(A\\) equal \\(1\\).  \n\nLet \\(J\\) denote the \\(n\\times n\\) all\u2011ones matrix and let \\(P\\) be the permutation matrix corresponding to \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. \\(p_{i,\\sigma(i)}=1\\) and all other entries are \\(0\\)). Then  \n\n\\[\nA = J-P. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  Each tile is a rank\u20111 matrix  \n\nA rectangular tile is determined by a set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (both non\u2011empty).  \nIts incidence matrix is  \n\n\\[\nT_{RC}=u_R\\,v_C^{\\!T},\n\\]\nwhere \\(u_R\\) is the column vector with \\((u_R)_i=1\\) iff \\(i\\in R\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise, and similarly \\(v_C\\) for the columns.  \nThus \\(\\operatorname{rank}(T_{RC})=1\\).\n\nIf Matilda uses \\(m\\) tiles, the matrices of the tiles sum to the covering matrix \\(A\\):\n\n\\[\nA = \\sum_{k=1}^{m} T_{R_kC_k}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks, (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}(T_{R_kC_k}) = m. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence any feasible tiling must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge \\operatorname{rank}(A). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  Rank of the matrix \\(J-P\\)\n\nWe claim that \\(J-P\\) is nonsingular, i.e. \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,1,\\dots ,1)^{T}\\). Because each row of a permutation matrix sums to \\(1\\),\n\n\\[\nP\\mathbf{1}= \\mathbf{1}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n(J-P)\\mathbf{1}=J\\mathbf{1}-P\\mathbf{1}=n\\mathbf{1}-\\mathbf{1}=(n-1)\\mathbf{1}\\neq\\mathbf{0}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(x\\) be any vector orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf{1}\\) (i.e. \\(\\mathbf{1}^{T}x=0\\)). Then \\(Jx=0\\) because each column of \\(J\\) is \\(\\mathbf{1}\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n(J-P)x = -Px.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is a permutation matrix, it is invertible; therefore \\(Px\\neq\\mathbf{0}\\) whenever \\(x\\neq\\mathbf{0}\\). Thus \\((J-P)x\\neq\\mathbf{0}\\) for every non\u2011zero \\(x\\) orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf{1}\\).\n\nTogether with (5) we see that the only solution of \\((J-P)x=\\mathbf{0}\\) is \\(x=\\mathbf{0}\\). Hence \\(J-P\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4) and (6) we obtain the **lower bound**\n\n\\[\nm\\ge n. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.  A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nChoose any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the uncovered squares will be \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single tile covering all columns except \\(\\sigma(i)\\). Formally, let  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad C_i=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe tile \\(T_{R_iC_i}\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle, it contains no uncovered square because the only column omitted from \\(C_i\\) is precisely the column of the uncovered square in that row.  \n\nThe \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct rows), and together they cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones. Hence a feasible tiling with \\(n\\) tiles exists, giving the **upper bound**\n\n\\[\nm\\le n. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nFrom (7) and (8) we conclude that the number of tiles required satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm=n.\n\\]\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]