\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Number the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n#### 1.  Encoding the covering by a matrix  \n\nDefine the binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{ij})_{1\\le i,j\\le n},\\qquad \na_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if the unit square in row }i\\text{ and column }j\\text{ is covered},\\\\[4pt]\n0 &\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of zeros of \\(A\\) consists of precisely one entry in each row and each column.  Hence there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\na_{i,\\sigma(i)}=0\\quad (i=1,\\dots ,n),\\qquad \na_{ij}=1\\ \\text{for all other }(i,j).\n\\]\n\nLet \\(J\\) denote the \\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix and let \\(P\\) be the permutation matrix corresponding to \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. \\(p_{i,\\sigma(i)}=1\\) and all other entries are \\(0\\)).  Then  \n\n\\[\nA = J-P. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  Each tile yields a rank‑1 matrix  \n\nA rectangular tile is determined by a non‑empty set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a non‑empty set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Its incidence matrix is  \n\n\\[\nT_{RC}=u_R\\,v_C^{\\!T},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(u_R\\) is the column vector with \\((u_R)_i=1\\) iff \\(i\\in R\\) (otherwise \\(0\\)), and \\(v_C\\) is defined analogously for the columns.  Hence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(T_{RC})=1\\).\n\nIf Matilda uses \\(m\\) tiles, the matrices of the tiles are pairwise disjoint (no unit square is covered twice), so their sum equals the covering matrix \\(A\\):\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{m}T_{R_kC_k}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks, (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}(T_{R_kC_k})\n=m. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible tiling must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  Rank of \\(J-P\\)  \n\nWe claim that \\(J-P\\) is invertible, i.e. \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n\\).\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf 1=(1,1,\\dots ,1)^{T}\\).  Since each row (and column) of a permutation matrix sums to \\(1\\),\n\n\\[\nP\\mathbf 1=\\mathbf 1.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n(J-P)\\mathbf 1=J\\mathbf 1-P\\mathbf 1=n\\mathbf 1-\\mathbf 1=(n-1)\\mathbf 1\\neq\\mathbf 0. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(x\\) be any vector orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf 1\\) (i.e. \\(\\mathbf 1^{T}x=0\\)).  Then \\(Jx=0\\) because each column of \\(J\\) equals \\(\\mathbf 1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n(J-P)x=-Px.\n\\]\n\nA permutation matrix \\(P\\) is invertible; therefore \\(Px\\neq\\mathbf 0\\) whenever \\(x\\neq\\mathbf 0\\).  Thus \\((J-P)x\\neq\\mathbf 0\\) for every non‑zero \\(x\\) orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf 1\\).\n\nTogether with (5) we see that the only solution of \\((J-P)x=\\mathbf 0\\) is \\(x=\\mathbf 0\\).  Hence \\(J-P\\) has trivial kernel and is nonsingular, so  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n.\n\\]\n\nCombining this with (4) yields the **lower bound**\n\n\\[\nm\\ge n. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.  A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nChoose any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the uncovered squares will be \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single tile covering all columns except \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  Formally, let  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad \nC_i=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe tile \\(T_{R_iC_i}\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle lying entirely in row \\(i\\); it does not cover the square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) because that column is omitted from \\(C_i\\).  \n\nSince the rows \\(R_i\\) are pairwise disjoint, the \\(n\\) tiles are disjoint.  Their union covers every unit square of the grid except precisely the \\(n\\) squares \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  Consequently this construction uses  \n\n\\[\nm=n\n\\]\n\ntiles and satisfies the required condition.  Hence we have the **upper bound**\n\n\\[\nm\\le n. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nFrom (7) and (8) we conclude that the number of tiles required satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm=n.\n\\]\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient. ∎"
