*left** part \\(\\bigcup_i L_i\\).  \nFor each column \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there exists a unique row, call it \\(r_{k+1}\\), such that  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(r_{k+1})=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((r_{k+1},k)\\) belongs to \\(L_{r_{k+1}}\\) (it is left of the hole in its row).  \n\n> **Claim.** Any rectangle that contains the cell \\((r_{k+1},k)\\) has its **rightmost** column \\(\\le k\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\((r_{k+1},k)\\) and its rightmost column were \\(b>k\\).  \nSince \\(b\\ge k+1\\) and \\(\\pi(r_{k+1})=k+1\\), the column \\(k+1\\) lies inside the column interval of \\(R\\).  \nConsequently the hole \\((r_{k+1},k+1)\\in U\\) would also belong to \\(R\\), contradicting the rule that a tile may not contain a hole. ∎  \n\nHence every rectangle covering a cell of column \\(k\\) in the left part must have rightmost column at most \\(k\\).  \nAssign each such rectangle to its rightmost column.  \nBecause column \\(k\\) contains the cell \\((r_{k+1},k)\\), there must be at least one rectangle assigned to \\(k\\).  \nTherefore the left part requires **at least \\(n-1\\) distinct rectangles**.\n\nThe same argument applied to the **right** part \\(\\bigcup_i R_i\\) (now looking at columns \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the unique row with hole in column \\(k-1\\)) shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover the right part.\n\nA rectangle cannot contain a hole, so a rectangle belonging to the left part cannot simultaneously belong to the right part. Consequently the total number of tiles in any tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the **identity permutation** \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the holes are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left part.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi(i)=i>k\\) for every \\(i>k\\), all cells of \\(V_k\\) lie in the left part and no hole belongs to \\(V_k\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover the entire left part.\n\n*Right part.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\pi(i)=i<j\\) for every \\(j>i\\), all cells of \\(H_i\\) lie in the right part and contain no hole.  \nThe rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover the entire right part.\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\n\\[\n\\{V_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\n\\]\n\nare disjoint, each lies on grid lines, none contains a hole, and they cover every square of the board except the \\(n\\) diagonal holes.  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (1) shows that **no** tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
