_k} = k, the hole is at column k+1. So column k+1 is the hole column for that row. So c_k is the last column left of hole.\n\nLemma 1: The cell c_k must belong to a left tile whose rightmost column is exactly k; distinct k force distinct left tiles.\n\nProof: Since tile must be left of holes for all rows it covers, it cannot include column k+1 (hole column). So tile's rightmost column ≤ k. Since tile includes column k (c_k), its rightmost column ≥ k. So rightmost column = k. So each left tile covering c_k has rightmost column exactly k.\n\nNow they argue that a left tile with rightmost column k cannot also cover c_{k'} for k'≠k. Suppose tile T has rightmost column k and also covers c_{k'} with k' < k. Then T must include row r_{k'}. But row r_{k'} has only k' columns to left of its hole, i.e., L_{r_{k'}} = k' < k. Since tile T's rightmost column is k, it would require that in row r_{k'}, the tile includes columns up to k, which would be beyond its left side (since hole is at column k'+1, and tile would include column k'+1? Actually, we need to check: tile T's columns are a contiguous interval from some leftmost column a to rightmost column k. Since tile is left tile, its columns must be ≤ L_i for each row i it covers. For row r_{k'}, L_{r_{k'}} = k', so the tile's columns must be ≤ k'. But tile's rightmost column is k > k', so it cannot be left tile covering row r_{k'}. So contradiction. So distinct k give distinct left tiles. So there are at least n-1 left tiles.\n\nSimilarly for right region: define s_k as unique row where R_{s_k} = k. Then d_k = (s_k, n - k + 1) is leftmost column of right region in that row (since hole is at column n - k). Actually hole column is pi(s_k) = n - k, so right region columns start at n - k + 1. So d_k is leftmost square in right region for that row. Lemma 2 similarly shows each right tile covering d_k must have leftmost column exactly n - k + 1, and distinct k give distinct right tiles. So at least n-1 right tiles.\n\nThus total at l