\((i,i)\\).  \nHence it suffices to determine the minimum for the board in which precisely the diagonal squares are uncovered.\n\nFrom now on we assume the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n#### 3.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nDefine, for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\times\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\n\\text{(size }k\\times (n-k)\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of pairwise disjoint rectangles; a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs exactly to \\(R_i\\).  \nThus \\(\\bigcup_{k=1}^{n-1}R_k\\) covers all squares above the diagonal.\n\nSimilarly, for each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{k,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\text{(size }(n-k+1)\\times (k-1)\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{S_k\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) consists of pairwise disjoint rectangles; a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) belongs exactly to \\(S_j\\).  \nThus \\(\\bigcup_{k=2}^{n}S_k\\) covers all squares below the diagonal.\n\nThe two families are disjoint because one covers only squares with row index \\(<\\) column index and the other only squares with row index \\(>\\) column index.  \nTogether they cover every square of the board except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \nThe total number of rectangles is\n\n\\[\n| \\{R_k\\}|+| \\{S_k\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n#### 4.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe prove that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nThe argument is by induction on \\(n\\ge2\\).\n\n**Base case \\(n=2\\).**  \nThe board consists of the two squares \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nAny rectangle containing both would have row set \\(\\{1,2\\}\\) and column set \\(\\{1,2\\}\\), which contains the uncovered squares \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); this is forbidden.  \nThus each of the two squares must belong to a distinct rectangle, so at least two tiles are required.  \nSi