(a rectangle remains a rectangle after any permutation, and the \u201cat most one tile per unit square\u2019\u2019 condition is preserved).  Applying a column permutation \\(\\sigma\\) with \\(\\sigma(f(i))=i\\) for all \\(i\\) moves the uncovered squares to the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  Consequently it suffices to determine the minimum number of tiles when the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A tiling using \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\times\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na rectangle of size \\(k\\times (n-k)\\).  The family \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of pairwise disjoint rectangles; a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs exactly to \\(R_i\\) (since \\(i\\le k=i\\) and \\(j\\ge i+1\\)).  Hence \\(\\bigcup_{k=1}^{n-1}R_k\\) covers all squares strictly above the diagonal.\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{k,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\]\n\na rectangle of size \\((n-k+1)\\times(k-1)\\).  The family \\(\\{S_k\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) also consists of pairwise disjoint rectangles; a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) belongs exactly to \\(S_{j+1}\\) (since \\(i\\ge j+1\\) and \\(j\\le j\\)).  Thus \\(\\bigcup_{k=2}^{n}S_k\\) covers all squares strictly below the diagonal.\n\nEvery rectangle \\(R_k\\) contains only squares with row index \\(<\\) column index, while every rectangle \\(S_k\\) contains only squares with row index \\(>\\) column index; therefore the two families are disjoint.  Their union covers every unit square of the board except the diagonal squares in \\(D\\).\n\nThe total number of rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{R_k\\}|+|\\{S_k\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge2\\) that any tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board with the diagonal squares \\(D\\) uncovered uses at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n**Base case \\(n=2\\).**  \nThe board consists of the two off\u2011diagonal squares \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  Any rectangle containing both would have row set \\(\\{1,2\\}\\) and column set \\(\\{1,2\\}\\), thereby also containing the uncovered squares \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), which is forbidden.  Hence each of the two squares must belong to a distinct rectangle, so at least two tiles are required.  Since \\(2n-2=2\\), the claim holds.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the claim holds for size \\((n-1)\\times(n-1)\\).  Consider a tiling \\(\\mathcal T\\) of the \\(n\\times n\\) board with diagonal uncovered.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{T\\in\\mathcal T\\mid T\\text{ contains a square of row }n\\},\n\\qquad\nC=\\{T\\in\\mathcal T\\mid T\\text{ contains a square of column }n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the square \\((n,n)\\) is uncovered, a tile that meets row \\(n\\) cannot also meet column \\(n\\); likewise a tile that meets column \\(n\\) cannot meet row \\(n\\).  Hence \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\).\n\nRow \\(n\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) squares \\((n,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq n\\); each of them must belong to some tile of \\(R\\).  Consequently \\(R\\neq\\varnothing\\) and \\(|R|\\ge1\\).  An analogous argument shows \\(|C|\\ge1\\).\n\nRemove from \\(\\mathcal T\\) all tiles belonging to \\(R\\cup C\\) and delete row \\(n\\) and column \\(n\\) from the board.  The remaining tiles form a tiling \\(\\mathcal T'\\) of the \\((n-1)\\times(n-1)\\) board whose uncovered squares are exactly \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  By the induction hypothesis, \\(|\\mathcal T'|\\ge 2(n-1)-2=2n-4\\).\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T|=|R|+|C|+|\\mathcal T'|\\ge 1+1+(2n-4)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling of the \\(n\\times n\\) board with diagonal uncovered requires at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\nSince the number of tiles is invariant under permutations of rows and columns, the same lower bound holds for any placement of the uncovered squares (i.e. for any permutation \\(f\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Minimal number of tiles  \n\nBoth the explicit construction and the inductive lower bound give the same value \\(2n-2\\).  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board is\n\n\\[\n2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
