ses **at least**  \n\n\\[\n2t\\ge 28\n\\]\n\nrectangles. This establishes the lower bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit a partition with exactly \\(28\\) rectangles.\n\n*Step 1 – Choosing the sets \\(R_i\\).*  \nTake the ground set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\).  \nSelect \\(2025\\) distinct subsets each of size \\(7\\); this is possible because \\(\\binom{14}{7}=3432>2025\\).  \nAssign one of these subsets to each row \\(i\\) and denote it by \\(R_i\\).\n\n*Step 2 – Defining the column sets.*  \nFor each column \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_i = \\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\setminus R_i .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(R_i\\cap C_i=\\varnothing\\) for every \\(i\\).\n\n*Step 3 – Verifying the intersection property.*  \nBecause all \\(R_i\\) have the same cardinality, none of them can contain another; hence for any distinct \\(i,j\\) the sets are incomparable, i.e. there exists an element \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\nk\\in R_i\\setminus R_j .\n\\]\n\nFor such a \\(k\\) we have \\(k\\in R_i\\) and \\(k\\notin R_j\\), which means \\(k\\in R_i\\) and \\(k\\in C_j\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nR_i\\cap C_j\\neq\\varnothing\\qquad(i\\neq j),\n\\]\n\nso condition (5) holds.\n\n*Step 4 – Building the rectangles.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\) define the two rectangles described above:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal{R}_k^{(1)} &= \\{\\,\\text{rows }i\\mid k\\in R_i\\,\\}\\times\n                       \\{\\,\\text{columns }j\\mid k\\notin R_j\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal{R}_k^{(2)} &= \\{\\,\\text{rows }i\\mid k\\notin R_i\\,\\}\\times\n                       \\{\\,\\text{columns }j\\mid k\\in R_j\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth rectangles are non‑empty because each of the two families of rows (those containing \\(k\\) and those not containing \\(k\\)) is non‑empty (the chosen subsets are not all identical).  \n\n*Step 5 – Partition property.*  \nTake any covered square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\). 