_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a rectangle cannot contain both the row and the column of an uncovered square, condition (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nR_i\\cap C_{\\sigma(i)}=\\varnothing\\qquad\\text{for every }i. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConversely, any pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq\\sigma(i)\\) must lie in some rectangle, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nR_i\\cap C_j\\neq\\varnothing\\qquad\\text{whenever }j\\neq\\sigma(i). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRenaming the columns so that \\(\\sigma\\) becomes the identity (this does not affect the combinatorial structure), we may assume \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \nThus (3)\u2013(4) become\n\n\\[\nR_i\\cap C_i=\\varnothing ,\\qquad R_i\\cap C_j\\neq\\varnothing\\;(i\\neq j). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound on the number of rectangles\n\nFrom (5) we obtain immediately that the family \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) is an **antichain**:\n\nif \\(R_i\\subseteq R_j\\) for some \\(i\\neq j\\), then\n\\(R_i\\cap C_j\\subseteq R_j\\cap C_j=\\varnothing\\), contradicting the second part of (5).  \nHence no set \\(R_i\\) contains another.\n\nA classic result of Sperner states that the largest antichain of subsets of a \\(t\\)-element set has size \\(\\displaystyle\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}\\).  \nConsequently we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}\\ge n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) the smallest integer \\(t\\) satisfying (6) is \\(t=14\\) because  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{13}{6}=1716<2025\\le\\binom{14}{7}=3432 .\n\\]\n\nEach element \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) can give at most two rectangles:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_k^{(1)}&= \\bigl\\{\\,\\text{rows }i\\mid k\\in R_i\\,\\bigr\\}\\times\n            \\bigl\\{\\,\\text{columns }j\\mid k\\notin C_j\\,\\bigr\\},\\\\\nB_k^{(2)}&= \\bigl\\{\\,\\text{rows }i\\mid k\\notin R_i\\,\\bigr\\}\\times\n            \\bigl\\{\\,\\text{columns }j\\mid k\\in C_j\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(If for a particular \\(k\\) one of the two classes is empty, the corresponding rectangle is omitted.)  \nTherefore any feasible partition uses **at least**  \n\n\\[\n2t\\ge 28\n\\]\n\nrectangles. This establishes the lower bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit a partition with exactly \\(28\\) rectangles.\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 Choosing the sets \\(R_i\\).*  \nTake the ground set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\).  \nSelect \\(2025\\) distinct subsets each of size \\(7\\); this is possible because \\(\\binom{14}{7}=3432>2025\\).  \nAssign one of these subsets to each row \\(i\\) and denote it by \\(R_i\\).\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 Defining the column sets.*  \nFor each column \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_i = \\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\setminus R_i .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(R_i\\cap C_i=\\varnothing\\) for every \\(i\\).\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 Verifying the intersection property.*  \nBecause all \\(R_i\\) have the same cardinality, none of them can contain another; hence for any distinct \\(i,j\\) the sets are incomparable, i.e. there exists an element \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\nk\\in R_i\\setminus R_j .\n\\]\n\nFor such a \\(k\\) we have \\(k\\in R_i\\) and \\(k\\notin R_j\\), which means \\(k\\in R_i\\) and \\(k\\in C_j\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nR_i\\cap C_j\\neq\\varnothing\\qquad(i\\neq j),\n\\]\n\nso condition (5) holds.\n\n*Step 4 \u2013 Building the rectangles.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\) define the two rectangles described above:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal{R}_k^{(1)} &= \\{\\,\\text{rows }i\\mid k\\in R_i\\,\\}\\times\n                       \\{\\,\\text{columns }j\\mid k\\notin R_j\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal{R}_k^{(2)} &= \\{\\,\\text{rows }i\\mid k\\notin R_i\\,\\}\\times\n                       \\{\\,\\text{columns }j\\mid k\\in R_j\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth rectangles are non\u2011empty because each of the two families of rows (those containing \\(k\\) and those not containing \\(k\\)) is non\u2011empty (the chosen subsets are not all 