dots ,N\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,N\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(r_c\\) the unique row in which column \\(c\\) contains an uncovered square, i.e.  \n\\(r_c = p^{-1}(c)\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Lower bound \\( \\ge 2N-2\\)\n\nFix a column \\(c\\). The cells of column \\(c\\) are the unit squares \\((i,c)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,N\\).  \nExactly one of them, \\((r_c,c)\\), is uncovered. Consequently the covered cells of column \\(c\\) split into at most two *vertical intervals*:\n\n* the **upper interval** \\(U_c = \\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_c-1\\}\\) (empty if \\(r_c=1\\));\n* the **lower interval** \\(L_c = \\{(i,c)\\mid r_c+1\\le i\\le N\\}\\) (empty if \\(r_c=N\\)).\n\nConsider any rectangular tile \\(T\\) used in a tiling.  \nIf \\(T\\) contains a cell of column \\(c\\), then all cells of \\(T\\) in column \\(c\\) must lie either entirely above the uncovered cell \\((r_c,c)\\) or entirely below it; otherwise \\(T\\) would contain \\((r_c,c)\\), which is forbidden.  \nHence every tile that covers a cell of column \\(c\\) is completely contained in either \\(U_c\\) or \\(L_c\\).\n\nTherefore each non‑empty interval \\(U_c\\) and \\(L_c\\) requires at least one distinct tile.  \nCounting the intervals:\n\n* Exactly one column contains its uncovered cell in the **top row** (the column \\(c\\) with \\(p(1)=c\\)). For this column \\(U_c\\) is empty, so only \\(L_c\\) is needed.\n* Exactly one column contains its uncovered cell in the **bottom row** (the column \\(c\\) with \\(p(N)=c\\)). For this column \\(L_c\\) is empty, so only \\(U_c\\) is needed.\n* For each of the remaining \\(N-2\\) columns the uncovered cell lies in an interior row, so both \\(U_c\\) and \\(L_c\\) are non‑empty.\n\nConsequently the total number of required intervals, and thus the total number of tiles, satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Construction achieving \\(2N-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) define the following two vertical tiles:\n\n* If \\(r_c>1\\), let  \n  \\[\n  T_c^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_c-1\\},\n  \\]\n  a rectangle of size \\((r_c-1)\\times1\\).\n* If \\(r_c<N\\), let  \n  \\[\n  T_c^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid r_c+1\\le i\\le N\\},\n  \\]\n  a rectangle of size \\((N-r_c)\\times1\\).\n\nEach tile lies on grid lines, its interior consists solely of covered squares, and different tiles occupy distinct columns, so no two tiles overlap.  \nAll covered squares belong to exactly one of the tiles defined above, while the \\(N\\) uncovered squares \\((r_c,c)\\) are omitted by construction.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{N}\\bigl(\\mathbf 1_{\\,r_c>1}+\\mathbf 1_{\\,r_c<N}\\bigr)\n   =(N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nbecause exactly one column has \\(r_c=1\\) (the column of the hole in the top row) and exactly one column has \\(r_c=N\\) (the column of the hole in the bottom row).\n\nThus the construction uses precisely the lower‑bound number of tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Minimality\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2N-2\\) tiles, while the construction above attains this number. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2N-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Application to the given board\n\nFor the problem at hand \\(N=2025\\). Substituting into the formula gives  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the required minimum number of rectangular tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
