                "content": "**### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   The condition “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square’’ forces the uncovered squares to be a set of \\(n\\) cells, one in each row and each column; they form a permutation matrix.\n\n2. **Decomposition into left and right Young diagrams.**  \n   For a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) be \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n   The squares left of the hole in row \\(i\\) form a left‑justified Young diagram with row length \\(\\ell_i=p(i)-1\\).  \n   The squares right of the hole form a right‑justified Young diagram with row length \\(r_i=n-p(i)\\).  \n   The two diagrams are disjoint and together constitute the whole set of squares that must be tiled.\n\n3. **Lemma (Young diagram partition).**  \n   *Lemma.*  Let a Young diagram have row lengths \\(\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_n\\).  \n   If \\(d\\) is the number of distinct positive values among the \\(\\lambda_i\\), then any partition of the diagram into axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least \\(d\\) rectangles, and \\(d\\) rectangles are sufficient (group rows of equal length).\n\n   *Proof idea.*  The rightmost column of height \\(h\\) (where \\(h\\) is a distinct row‑length) cannot be merged with a column of a different height; thus each distinct height forces a new rectangle.  Induction on \\(d\\) yields the lower bound, while grouping equal‑length rows yields a construction attaining it.\n\n4. **Application of the lemma.**  \n   Because \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{p(i)-1\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); hence the left diagram has exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct positive row lengths.  The same holds for the right diagram \\(\\{n-p(i)\\}\\).  Consequently any tiling needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\).  Then the left diagram consists of rows \\(i\\;(i\\ge2)\\) of length \\(i-1\\); each row is tiled by a single \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle.  This gives \\(n-1\\) tiles.  The right diagram consists of rows \\(i\\;(i\\le n-1)\\) of length \\(n-i\\); each is tiled by a single \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle, giving another \\(n-1\\) tiles.  All \\(2n-2\\) tiles are disjoint and cover every square except the diagonal holes.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  We work with an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares whose rows are indexed by \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) and whose columns are indexed by \\(1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nEach row must contain exactly one uncovered square, and the same holds for each column.  \nHence there are exactly \\(n\\) uncovered squares, one in each row and one in each column.  \nConsequently there is a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nThe set \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions\n\nFor a fixed permutation \\(p\\) define, for each row \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{\\,j\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\,\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{\\,j\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe squares of the board that are not holes are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\{i\\}\\times L_i\\bigl)\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\{i\\}\\times R_i\\bigl).\n\\]\n\nThe two families are disjoint; we call them the **left region** and the **right region**.\nBoth are Young diagrams (Ferrers shapes) after a suitable 90° rotation of the right region.\n\nThe left region has row lengths  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=|L_i|=p(i)-1,\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nwhile the right region has row lengths  \n\n\\[\nr_i=|R_i|=n-p(i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma on rectangle partitions of a Young diagram  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\;\n\\text{Let }D\\text{ be a Young diagram with row lengths }\n\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_n\\;( \\lambda_i\\ge0).\n\\text{ Let }d\\text{ be the number of distinct positive values among }\n\\{\\lambda_i\\}.\n\\text{ Then any partition of }D\\text{ into axis‑parallel rectangles\nuses at least }d\\text{ rectangles, and }d\\text{ rectangles are sufficient.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the distinct positive lengths in decreasing order  \n\n\\[\n\\mu_1>\\mu_2>\\cdots>\\mu_d>0 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\) the column numbered \\(\\mu_k\\) (the \\(\\mu_k\\)-th column from the left)\ncontains squares in exactly the first \\(k\\) rows and no square in row \\(k+1\\)\n(because \\(\\lambda_{k+1}<\\mu_k\\)).  Hence column \\(\\mu_k\\) has height \\(k\\).\n\nConsider any rectangle in a partition that contains a square of column \\(\\mu_k\\).\nSince the column is contiguous from row \\(1\\) to row \\(k\\), the rectangle can\nextend only within those rows; it cannot contain a square of column \\(\\mu_{k'}\\)\nwith \\(k'\\neq k\\), because such a column has a different height and would force\nthe rectangle to include a missing square.  Consequently each distinct height\n\\(k\\) forces a distinct rectangle.  Thus at least \\(d\\) rectangles are needed.\n\nConversely, group together all rows whose length equals the same \\(\\mu_k\\).\nFor each \\(k\\) the rows of length \\(\\mu_k\\) together with the first\n\