mes1\\) (a vertical strip).  \nThe family \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and their union equals \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n**Right side.**  \nFor each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(i,k+1)\\mid i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_k\\) is a rectangle of dimensions \\(k\\times1\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{S_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and their union equals \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nThe \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_k\\}\\cup\\{S_k\\}\\) are axis‑parallel, non‑overlapping, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \nConsequently they satisfy the requirement that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square.\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Minimality\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, while the construction above uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.  \nHence the lower bound is tight and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
