lusion.** The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction attaining it show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this number is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the \\(n\\times n\\) grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles with the required property is equivalent to the following combinatorial model.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Reformulation as a permutation problem  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares are covered, and the tiles must partition this set\n\\[\nC:=\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle, i.e. a set of the form\n\\[\nR\\times C\\qquad\\text{with }R\\subseteq[n],\\;C\\subseteq[n]\\text{ intervals},\n\\]\nand the condition “no tile contains an uncovered square’’ is exactly\n\\[\n(R\\times C)\\cap U=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem asks for the smallest number of rectangles satisfying (1) whose union is \\(C\\) and which are pairwise disjoint.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Decomposition into left and right regions  \n\nDefine\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nClearly \\(C=L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) the *left length* is\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=|\\{j\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\}|=\\sigma(i)-1,\n\\]\nand the *right length* is\n\\[\nr_i:=|\\{j\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}|=n-\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are exactly the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); similarly the numbers \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\) are also \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Hence each of the values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) occurs **once** as a positive left length and **once** as a positive right length.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left region  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{Any partition of }L\\text{ into axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) be a partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles.  \nFor a rectangle \\(Q\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) denote by\n\\[\nc(Q):=\\max\\{j\\mid (i,j)\\in Q\\}\n\\]\nits rightmost column.  Because \\(Q\\subseteq L\\), every row \\(i\\) belonging to \\(Q\\) satisfies\n\\[\nc(Q)\\le \\ell_i .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFix a positive integer \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell_i=k\\) for exactly one row \\(i\\) (the row with \\(\\sigma(i)=k+1\\)), the cell \\((i,k)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and is the **rightmost** cell of that row’s left part.  By (2) the rectangle covering \\((i,k)\\) must have rightmost column \\(c(Q)=k\\); a larger value would force the rectangle to contain a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>k\\), which is not in \\(L\\).  \n\nConsequently each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) appears as the rightmost column of at least one rectangle of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  Different values of \\(k\\) cannot be the rightmost column of the same rectangle, because a rectangle has a single rightmost column.  Hence \\(|\\mathcal{P}|\\ge n-1\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right region  \n\nExactly the same argument applied to the right region \\(R\\) (using the right lengths \\(r_i\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{Any partition of }R\\text{ into rectangles uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nEvery admissible tiling of the whole board induces a partition of \\(L\\) and a partition of \\(R\\).  By Lemmas 1 and 2 the numbers of rectangles covering \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are each at least \\(n-1\\).  Therefore any tiling uses at least\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  The uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*Lower triangular part.*  For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define\n\\[\nL_k:=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\\(L_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\); the sets \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is exactly \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\).\n\n*Upper triangular part.*  For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define\n\\[\nU_k:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(U_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\(k\\times (n-k)\\); the sets \\(U_1,\\dots ,U_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\).\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\\[\n\\{L_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{U_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\ncover every square of \\(C\\) exactly once and avoid all diagonal squares.  Hence they constitute a valid placement of \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (3) and the explicit construction with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
