upper’’ rectangles and \\(n-1\\) “lower’’ rectangles, altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and they cover every unit square except the diagonal holes.\n\n5. **Universality of the construction.**  \n   If the holes are placed according to an arbitrary permutation \\(q\\), apply the column permutation that sends column \\(j\\) to column \\(q(j)\\).  The above tiling of the identity case is carried over to a tiling of the general case, still using exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The board consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation  \n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nEquivalently, if we denote by \\(q\\) the inverse permutation,\n\\[\nq(j)=p^{-1}(j)\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n),\n\\]\nthen the hole in column \\(j\\) lies at \\((q(j),j)\\).\n\nA rectangular tile is a set of the form  \n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid i_1\\le i\\le i_2,\\;j_1\\le j\\le j_2\\}\n\\]\nwith integers \\(1\\le i_1\\le i_2\\le n,\\;1\\le j_1\\le j_2\\le n\\).  \nA tile may be placed only if none of its unit squares is a hole; i.e. for every column \\(j\\) occurring in the tile we must have either  \n\\(i_2<q(j)\\) (the tile lies completely **above** the hole in that column) or  \n\\(i_1>q(j)\\) (the tile lies completely **below** the hole in that column).  \n\nThus each tile is a *complete bipartite subgraph* of the bipartite graph whose vertex classes are the rows and the columns, and whose edges are the non‑hole squares.  In graph‑theoretic language a tile corresponds to a pair \\((R,C)\\) with\n\\[\nR\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad R\\cap C=\\varnothing,\n\\]\nand it covers precisely the edges \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}\\).  \nThe problem is therefore to partition the edge set of the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{n,n}\\) with the \\(n\\) edges \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) removed, into such complete bipartite subgraphs.  The minimum number of subgraphs needed is the *rectangle covering number* of the matrix \\(J-I_n\\) (the all‑1 matrix with a zero on each diagonal entry).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Lower bound\n\nConsider the following set of off‑diagonal cells:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}\n   =\\{(i,1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(1,j)\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nIt contains exactly \\(2n-2\\) cells.  \nSuppose a rectangle \\((R,C)\\) with \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  \nIf it contains \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,j)\\) (with \\(i>1,\\;j>1\\)), then \\(1\\in C\\) (because the first cell lies in column 1) and \\(1\\in R\\) (because the second cell lies in row 1).  Hence \\(1\\in R\\cap C\\), contradicting the requirement \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\).  \nThus **no** rectangle can contain two different elements of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  Consequently each element of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) must be covered by a distinct rectangle, and any covering of all non‑hole squares uses at least \\(|\\mathcal{F}|=2n-2\\) rectangles.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{t_{\\min}\\ge 2n-2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound – a construction attaining the bound\n\nFirst treat the identity permutation \\(q(j)=j\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \nFor every column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle\n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers all cells **above** the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \nFor every column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle\n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers all cells **below** the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \n\nThe two families are disjoint, each rectangle consists solely of non‑hole squares, and together they cover every off‑diagonal cell exactly once.  Their total number is\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow let the holes be placed according to an arbitrary permutation \\(q\\).  \nApply the column permutation \\(\\sigma\\) defined by \\(\\sigma(j)=q(j)\\).  \nThe board with holes at \\((q(j),j)\\) is obtained from the identity board by permuting the columns according to \\(\\sigma\\).  \nIf we apply the same column permutation to the tiling described above, each rectangle remains a rectangle (its column interval is permuted) and still avoids all holes, because the permutation sends the diagonal holes to the prescribed holes.  Hence the same number \\(2n-2\\) of rectangles tiles the board for **any** permutation of the holes.\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, giving\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{t_{\\min}\\le 2n-2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) and (3) we obtain equality:\n\\[\nt_{\\min}=2n-2 .\n\\]\nFor the given size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
