ts ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad p(i)=\\text{column of the uncovered cell in row }i .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a set of \\(n\\) cells, one in each row and each column.  \n\nIf we permute the rows according to a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) and the columns according to a permutation \\(\\tau\\), the board is still an \\(n\\times n\\) grid, the condition “one uncovered cell in each row and column’’ is preserved, and a family of tiles is transformed into a family of the same cardinality (each tile is moved rigidly).  \nConsequently the minimum possible number of tiles depends only on the *relative* positions of the uncovered cells, not on the particular permutation \\(p\\).\n\nChoosing \\(\\sigma=p\\) and \\(\\tau=\\operatorname{id}\\) sends the uncovered cells to the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \nHence **without loss of generality we may assume that the uncovered cells are exactly the diagonal cells**  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A set of forced cells  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_1=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\\qquad   \nS_2=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\nand let  \n\n\\[\nS=S_1\\cup S_2 .\n\\]\n\nAll cells of \\(S\\) are covered (they are not in \\(D\\)).  \nThe set \\(S\\) contains exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma – a rectangle cannot contain two cells of \\(S\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\\text{Let }R=I\\times J\\text{ be a rectangle ( }I,J\\text{ intervals of rows and columns) that contains no diagonal cell. \n\\text{ Then }|R\\cap S|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\).  \nThere are three possibilities.\n\n1. **Both cells belong to \\(S_1\\).**  \n   Then they are \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\).  \n   Suppose \\(i<k\\).  \n   Because the column interval \\(J\\) is contiguous and contains the columns \\(i+1\\) and \\(k+1\\), it also contains the column \\(k\\).  \n   Since the row interval \\(I\\) contains the row \\(k\\), the cell \\((k,k)\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  \n   But \\((k,k)\\in D\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(R\\) avoids all diagonal cells.\n\n2. **Both cells belong to \\(S_2\\).**  \n   This is symmetric to the previous case: the two cells are \\((i+1,i)\\) and \\((k+1,k)\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \n   The row interval contains rows \\(i+1\\) and \\(k+1\\); therefore it also contains row \\(k+1\\).  \n   The column interval contains columns \\(i\\) and \\(k\\); being contiguous it also contains column \\(k\\).  \n   Hence \\((k+1,k)\\) is already one of the chosen cells, but the rectangle also contains \\((k+1,k+1)\\), a diagonal cell – a contradiction.\n\n3. **One cell from \\(S_1\\) and one from \\(S_2\\).**  \n   Say the cells are \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k+1,k)\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\).  \n   If \\(i<k\\) then, as in case 1, the column interval contains column \\(k\\) and the row interval contains row \\(i\\); consequently the cell \\((i,k)\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \n   Because \\(k\\ge i+1\\), we have \\(k\\ge i+1\\) and thus the column \\(k\\) lies to the right of the hole \\((i,i)\\); therefore \\((i,k)\\) is a covered cell, not a hole.  \n   However the column interval also contains column \\(k\\) and the row interval contains row \\(k+1\\); thus the cell \\((k+1,k)\\) (already one of the chosen cells) is present, and the column interval also contains column \\(k+1\\).  \n   Since the row interval contains row \\(k+1\\), the cell \\((k+1,k+1)\\) belongs to \\(R\\), which is a diagonal cell – a contradiction.\n\nAll three cases lead to a contradiction, so a rectangle avoiding the diagonal can contain **at most one** element of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile is a rectangle that avoids the