gonal, hence by the Lemma it can cover at most one cell of \\(S\\).  \nAll cells of \\(S\\) must be covered, and \\(|S|=2(n-1)\\).  \nTherefore any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nPlace the uncovered cells on the diagonal, i.e. \\(D=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) put a tile  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle covering the whole right part of row \\(i\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) put a tile  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle covering the whole lower part of column \\(j\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint: a horizontal tile lies entirely in a single row, a vertical tile lies entirely in a single column, and the only cells belonging to both a horizontal and a vertical tile would be the intersections \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) and \\(j\\le n-1\\); but such an intersection would be \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\le i\\) (if it were in a horizontal tile) **and** \\(i\\le j\\) (if it were in a vertical tile), which is impossible.  \n\nEvery non‑diagonal cell belongs either to the right of its row’s diagonal (hence to some \\(H_i\\)) or below its column’s diagonal (hence to some \\(V_j\\)).  Consequently the union of all \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) covers exactly the set of all covered cells.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{H_i\\}|+|\\{V_j\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound of Section 4 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of Section 5 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is