"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation reduction.**  \n   The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: one uncovered cell in each row and each column. By permuting rows and columns we may assume the uncovered cells lie on the main diagonal  \n   \\[\n   D=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\\qquad n=2025 .\n   \\]  \n   This transformation does not change the number of tiles, so a lower bound proved for the diagonal case holds for any configuration.\n\n2. **Forced cells and a covering lemma.**  \n   Define the set of cells adjacent to the diagonal  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{(i+1,i)\\mid1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n   \\]  \n   which contains \\(2(n-1)\\) cells.  \n   **Lemma.** Any axis\u2011parallel rectangle that avoids all diagonal cells can contain **at most one** element of \\(S\\).  \n   *Proof idea:* For a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) with \\(I,J\\) integer intervals, the condition \u201c\\(R\\cap D=\\varnothing\\)\u201d implies \\(I\\cap J=\\varnothing\\). Hence either \\(\\max I<\\min J\\) (all rows are smaller than all columns) or \\(\\min I>\\max J\\) (all rows are larger than all columns). In the first case only cells of the form \\((i,i+1)\\) can belong to \\(R\\); two such cells would force a diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) to lie in \\(R\\), a contradiction. The second case is symmetric. \u220e\n\n3. **Lower bound.**  \n   Every tile is a rectangle that avoids the uncovered diagonal cells, so by the Lemma each tile covers at most one cell of \\(S\\). Since all cells of \\(S\\) must be covered, any tiling uses at least \\(|S|=2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   With the diagonal uncovered, place the following \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles:\n\n   *For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) a horizontal tile*  \n   \\[\n   H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n   \\]  \n   a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle covering the part of row \\(i\\) to the right of the diagonal.\n\n   *For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) a vertical tile*  \n   \\[\n   V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n   \\]  \n   an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle covering the part of column \\(j\\) below the diagonal.\n\n   The families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are disjoint because a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs only to \\(H_i\\) and a cell with \\(i>j\\) belongs only to \\(V_j\\). Every non\u2011diagonal cell satisfies either \\(i<j\\) or \\(i>j\\), hence is covered exactly once. The total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number of tiles, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA *cell* is a unit square of the \\(n\\times n\\) board.  \nA *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it consists of a set of whole cells and may be of any size \\(a\\times b\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\).  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint (no cell is covered twice).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal configuration  \n\nFor a placement of the uncovered cells denote by  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad p(i)=\\text{column of the uncovered cell in row }i .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered cells are \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the map \\(p\\) is a permutation because each column also contains exactly one uncovered cell.\n\nIf we permute the rows according to a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) and the columns according to a permutation \\(\\tau\\), the board remains an \\(n\\times n\\) grid, the condition \u201cone uncovered cell in each row and column\u2019\u2019 is preserved, and any family of tiles is transformed into a family of the same cardinality (each tile is moved rigidly).  \n\nChoosing \\(\\sigma=p\\) and \\(\\tau=\\operatorname{id}\\) sends the uncovered cells to the diagonal cells  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently, **without loss of generality we may assume that the uncovered cells are exactly the diagonal cells**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. A set of forced cells  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_1=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\\qquad   \nS_2=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\nand let  \n\n\\[\nS=S_1\\cup S_2 .\n\\]\n\nAll cells of \\(S\\) are not on the diagonal, hence they must be covered.  \n\\(|S|=2(n-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lemma \u2013 a rectangle avoiding the diagonal contains at most one cell of \\(S\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\\text{Let }R=I\\times J\\text{ be a rectangle (}I,J\\text{ integer intervals) such that }R\\cap D=\\varnothing.\n\\text{ Then }|R\\cap S|\\le1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(R\\cap D=\\varnothing\\), for every integer \\(k\\) we cannot have simultaneously \\(k\\in I\\) and \\(k\\in J\\); otherwise the diagonal cell \\((k,k)\\) would belong to \\(R\\). Hence the intervals \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are disjoint: \\(I\\cap J=\\varnothing\\).\n\nBecause \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals, disjointness implies exactly one of the two possibilities:\n\n1. \\(\\max I<\\min J\\).  \n   Then every cell \\((i,j)\\in R\\) satisfies \\(i<j\\). Consequently a cell of \\(S\\) that can lie in \\(R\\) must belong to \\(S_1\\) (of the form \\((i,i+1)\\)).  \n\n   Suppose two distinct cells \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\) of \\(S_1\\) lie in \\(R\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \n   Since \\(i,k\\in I\\) and \\(I\\) is an interval, it also contains the integer \\(i+1\\) (because \\(i+1\\le k\\)).  \n   Moreover \\(i+1\\in J\\) because \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\).  \n   Hence \\(i+1\\in I\\cap J\\), which would place the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) inside \\(R\\), contradicting \\(R\\cap D=\\varnothing\\).  \n   Therefore at most one element of \\(S_1\\) can belong to \\(R\\).\n\n2. \\(\\min I>\\max J\\).  \n   This is symmetric: every cell of \\(R\\) satisfies \\(i>j\\), so only cells of \\(S_2\\) (of the form \\((i+1,i)\\)) could lie in \\(R\\).  \n   If two such cells \\((i+1,i)\\) and \\((k+1,k)\\) with \\(i<k\\) were in \\(R\\), then \\(i+1\\in I\\cap J\\) (by the same interval argument), again forcing a diagonal cell into \\(R\\).  \n   Hence at most one element of \\(S_2\\) can belong to \\(R\\).\n\nIn either case \\(|R\\cap S|\\le1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile is a rectangle that avoids all uncovered cells, i.e. avoids the diagonal \\(D\\). By the Lemma, each tile can cover at most one cell of \\(S\\). Since all \\(|S|=2(n-1)\\) cells of \\(S\\) must be covered, any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Construction attaining the bound  \n\nPlace the uncovered cells on the diagonal \\(D\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle covering the part of row \\(i\\) to the right of the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nan \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle covering the part of column \\(j\\) below the diagonal cell \\((j,j)\\).\n\n**Disjointness.**  \nA cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to the horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) and to no vertical tile, because a vertical tile \\(V_j\\) contains only cells with row index \\(\\ge j+1>i\\).  \nA cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) belongs to the vertical tile \\(V_j\\) and to no horizontal tile, because a horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) contains only cells with column index \\(\\ge i+1>j\\).  \nThus the families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nEvery non\u2011diagonal cell satisfies either \\(i<j\\) or \\(i>j\\); consequently it lies in exactly one of the tiles described above. The diagonal cells remain uncovered. Hence the union of all \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) covers precisely the set of all covered cells.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{H_i\\}|+|\\{V_j\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound of Section\u202f4 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe construction of Section\u202f5 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
