".\n\n**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{2025}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares:** The condition \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to form a permutation matrix \\(P\\) (there is a permutation \\(\\pi\\) with uncovered squares \\((i,\\pi(i))\\)).  \n2. **Matrix formulation:** Let \\(J\\) be the \\(n\\times n\\) all\u2011ones matrix (\\(n=2025\\)). The indicator matrix of the covered squares is \\(M=J-P\\).  \n3. **Lower bound via rank:**  \n   * A rectangular tile covering rows \\(I\\) and columns \\(J\\) corresponds to the rank\u20111 matrix \\(u_Iv_J^{\\mathsf T}\\).  \n   * If \\(k\\) tiles cover the board without overlap, then \\(M\\) is the sum of \\(k\\) rank\u20111 matrices, so \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le k\\).  \n   * For any permutation matrix \\(P\\), \\(M=J-P\\) is invertible (its eigenvalues are \\(n-\\lambda\\) where \\(\\lambda\\) are the eigenvalues of \\(P\\); none equals \\(0\\) for \\(n\\ge2\\)). Hence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n\\).  \n   * Therefore any tiling needs at least \\(n\\) tiles.  \n4. **Construction attaining the bound:** Choose any permutation \\(\\pi\\). For each row \\(i\\) place a single \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile covering all squares of that row except the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\). The \\(n\\) tiles are disjoint, cover all other squares, and leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and column.  \n5. **Conclusion:** The lower bound \\(n\\) and the explicit construction with \\(n\\) tiles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the set of rows by \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the set of columns also by \\([n]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the uncovered squares form a *permutation matrix*.  \nThus there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\([n]\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set of covered squares is  \n\n\\[\nC=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Matrix representation  \n\nLet \\(J\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times n}\\) be the all\u2011ones matrix and let \\(P\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times n}\\) be the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\) (i.e. \\(P_{i,\\pi(i)}=1\\) and all other entries are \\(0\\)).  \n\nThe indicator matrix of the covered squares is  \n\n\\[\nM=J-P .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(M_{i,j}=1\\) iff \\((i,j)\\in C\\) and \\(M_{i,j}=0\\) iff \\((i,j)\\in U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nConsider a rectangular tile \\(T\\) that occupies a set of rows \\(R\\subseteq[n]\\) and a set of columns \\(C_T\\subseteq[n]\\).  \nThe indicator matrix of \\(T\\) is  \n\n\\[\nA_T = u_R\\,v_{C_T}^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(u_R\\) (resp. \\(v_{C_T}\\)) is the column vector whose entries are \\(1\\) exactly on the indices belonging to \\(R\\) (resp. \\(C_T\\)).  \nHence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A_T)=1\\).\n\nSuppose a tiling uses \\(k\\) tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_k\\).  \nBecause the tiles are disjoint and together cover exactly the set \\(C\\),\n\n\\[\nM = \\sum_{t=1}^{k} A_{T_t}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor any matrices \\(X,Y\\) we have \\(\\operatorname{rank}(X+Y)\\le\\operatorname{rank}(X)+\\operatorname{rank}(Y)\\); by induction,\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le\\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(A_{T_t}) = k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\)\n\nThe matrix \\(J\\) has rank \\(1\\).  \nA permutation matrix \\(P\\) is orthogonal, hence invertible, and has rank \\(n\\).  \n\nThe eigenvalues of \\(P\\) are roots of unity; denote them by \\(\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_n\\) (one of them is \\(1\\)).  \nSince \\(J\\) has eigenvalue \\(n\\) for the eigenvector \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\) and eigenvalue \\(0\\) on the orthogonal complement, the eigenvalues of \\(M=J-P\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\mu_i = n-\\lambda_i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge 2\\) and each \\(\\lambda_i\\) satisfies \\(|\\lambda_i|=1\\), we have \\(\\mu_i\\neq0\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nHence \\(M\\) is invertible and\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) yields the lower bound\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nFix any permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\([n]\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a tile\n\n\\[\nT_i = \\{i\\}\\times\\bigl([n]\\setminus\\{\\pi(i)\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n\\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle; its indicator matrix is \\(u_{\\{i\\}}v_{[n]\\setminus\\{\\pi(i)\\}}^{\\mathsf T}\\).  \n\nThe tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows.  \nTheir union covers precisely the set \\(C\\) of all squares except the \\(n\\) squares \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \n\nConsequently each row \\(i\\) has exactly one uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\), and each column \\(j\\) has exactly one uncovered square \\((\\pi^{-1}(j),j)\\).  \n\nThus we have a valid tiling using exactly \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (5) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles, while the construction of \u00a75 achieves \\(n\\) tiles.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
