ould contain the row \\(\\sigma(j)\\); because column \\(j\\) lies inside the rectangle’s column interval, the hole \\((\\sigma(j),j)\\) would also lie inside the rectangle – a contradiction.  \n   Therefore each rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(A\\); covering all of \\(A\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n\n   – An analogous argument applied to the set of squares directly **below** each uncovered square,  \n   \\[\n   B=\\{(\\sigma(j)+1,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n,\\ \\sigma(j)<n\\},\n   \\]  \n   shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary.  \n\n   Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are disjoint, any tiling must contain at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient, and the contradiction argument shows that fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles are impossible. Hence the minimum number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025,\n\\qquad \n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the bijection defined by the requirement that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies in column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(j)=\\pi^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nthe row containing the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).  \nThe board is the set  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nand the squares that must be covered are  \n\n\\[\nC=B\\setminus H.\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose vertices are grid points; equivalently a set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\}\n\\]\n\nfor some integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint and none may contain a point of \\(H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound – an explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) we consider the two vertical strips determined by the uncovered square in that column.\n\n*If \\(\\sigma(j)>1\\)*, define  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<\\sigma(j)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThis is a (possibly empty) rectangle of height \\(\\sigma(j)-1\\) situated entirely **above** the uncovered square \\((\\sigma(j),j)\\); consequently it contains no element of \\(H\\).\n\n*If \\(\\sigma(j)<n\\)*, define  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{down}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(j)<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThis rectangle lies entirely **below** the uncovered square in column \\(j\\) and also avoids \\(H\\).\n\nThe two rectangles belonging to the same column are disjoint, because the uncovered square separates them.  \nAll rectangles are vertical, hence any two belonging to different columns are also disjoint.  \n\nNow count the non‑empty rectangles.  \nExactly one column contains the uncovered square in the top row (the column \\(j\\) with \\(\\sigma(j)=1\\)); for this column \\(T^{\\text{up}}_j\\) is empty.  \nSimilarly, exactly one column contains the uncovered square in the bottom row (\\(\\sigma(j)=n\\)); for this column \\(T^{\\text{down}}_j\\) is empty.  \nEvery other column contributes one non‑empty “up’’ rectangle and one non‑empty “down’’ rectangle.  \nHence the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nSince the union of all \\(T^{\\text{up}}_j\\) and \\(T^{\\text{down}}_j\\) is precisely \\(C\\), this construction yields a valid tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nWe prove two separate lower bounds, one for the squares **above** the uncovered ones and one for the squares **below** them.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.1 (above squares).**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(\\sigma(j)-1,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n,\\ \\sigma(j)>1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(A\\)