                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{2025}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. By permuting rows and columns we may assume the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) (here \\(n=2025\\)).  \n2. The set of covered cells is then described by the binary matrix \\(A=J-I\\) (all 1’s except 0’s on the diagonal).  \n3. A rectangular tile that covers rows \\(R\\) and columns \\(C\\) with \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\) corresponds to the rank‑1 binary matrix \\(uv^{\\!T}\\) where \\(u_i=1\\iff i\\in R\\) and \\(v_j=1\\iff j\\in C\\).  \n4. A tiling is a partition of the 1‑entries of \\(A\\) into such rank‑1 matrices; hence if \\(k\\) tiles are used we have \\(A=\\sum_{t=1}^{k}u_t v_t^{\\!T}\\).  \n5. Since \\(\\operatorname{rank}(u_t v_t^{\\!T})\\le1\\) for each \\(t\\), subadditivity of rank gives \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le k\\).  \n6. The matrix \\(A=J-I\\) has rank \\(n\\) (its rows are linearly independent, or its eigenvalues are \\(n-1\\) and \\(-1\\) with multiplicities \\(1\\) and \\(n-1\\)).  \n7. Consequently \\(k\\ge n\\).  \n8. A construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles is obtained by placing, for each \\(i\\), a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile covering row \\(i\\) and all columns except column \\(i\\).  \n9. Hence the minimum number of tiles equals \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA unit square is identified by its row index \\(i\\) and column index \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to a fixed pattern of uncovered squares\nThe condition “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square’’ means that the set of uncovered squares forms a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(\\sigma:[n]\\to[n]\\) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n\nRenaming the rows by the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. applying the same permutation to the row indices) and simultaneously renaming the columns by the same permutation sends the uncovered squares to the diagonal positions \\((i,i)\\). This relabelling does not change the number of tiles needed.  \nHence we may assume from now on that the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Matrix formulation\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{ij}),\\qquad a_{ij}=\n\\begin{cases}\n0 &\\text{if }i=j,\\\\[2pt]\n1 &\\text{if }i\\neq j .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A=J-I\\), where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.  \nA rectangular tile whose set of rows is \\(R\\subseteq[n]\\) and set of columns is \\(C\\subseteq[n]\\) (with \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\)) covers exactly the entries \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in R,\\ j\\in C\\).  \n\nIntroduce the binary column vectors  \n\n\\[\nu^{(R)}_i=\\mathbf 1_{\\{i\\in R\\}},\\qquad \nv^{(C)}_j=\\mathbf 1_{\\{j\\in C\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe coverage matrix of the tile is the outer product  \n\n\\[\nB^{(R,C)} = u^{(R)}\\,(v^{(C)})^{\\!T},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rank‑1 matrix (or the zero matrix if one of the vectors is zero).  \nBecause \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\), we have \\(u^{(R)}_i v^{(C)}_i =0\\) for every \\(i\\); consequently \\(B^{(R,C)}\\) has zeros on the diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. From a tiling to a matrix decomposition\nSuppose a tiling uses \\(k\\) tiles \\(\\{(R_t,C_t)\\}_{t=1}^{k}\\).  \nThe corresponding matrices \\(B_t:=B^{(R_t,C_t)}\\) are pairwise entry‑wise disjoint (no unit square is covered twice). Hence\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{t=1}^{k} B_t .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(B_t\\) has rank at most \\(1\\). By the subadditivity of matrix rank,\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(B_t)\\le k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\)\nThe rows of \\(A\\) are linearly independent.  \nIndeed, suppose \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i\\text{(row }i)=0\\).  \nLooking at column \\(j\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\neq j}\\alpha_i =0\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the equations for \\(j\\) and \\(j'\\) yields \\(\\alpha_{j'}-\\alpha_{j}=0\\); thus all \\(\\alpha_i\\) are equal, say \\(\\alpha_i=\\alpha\\).  \nThen \\((n-1)\\alpha=0\\) gives \\(\\alpha=0\\). Hence the only linear relation is the trivial one, so the rows are independent and \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\).\n\n(Equivalently, \\(A\\) has eigenvalues \\(n-1\\) (multiplicity 1) and \\(-1\\) (multiplicity \\(n-1\\)), so its rank is \\(n\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Lower bound on the number of tiles\nFrom (2) and \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling satisfying the problem’s condition must use at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\nFor each \\(i\\in[n]\\) place the tile  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad C_i=[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically this is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle covering row \\(i\\) except the uncovered cell \\((i,i)\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every off‑diagonal cell, i.e. they realise the decomposition (1) with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Conclusion\nBoth the lower bound (3) and the explicit construction show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(n\\).  \nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\), the answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
