eatest row index occurring in \\(R\\) is at most \\(i\\).  But the cell \\((k,k+1)\\) has row index \\(k>i\\), a contradiction.  \u220e\n\nThe set \\(S\\) has cardinality \\(n-1\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 each rectangle of a tiling of \\(U\\) can contain at most one element of \\(S\\); consequently any tiling of \\(U\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n#### 4. Lower bound for the lower triangular part  \n\nDefine the *sub\u2011diagonal*\n\\[\nT=\\{(i,i-1):2\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Lemma\u202f2.**  \nLet \\(R\\) be a rectangle covering cells of \\(L\\).  Then \\(R\\) contains at most one cell of \\(T\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(R\\) contained \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((k,k-1)\\) with \\(i<k\\), then its column interval would have to contain both \\(i-1\\) and \\(k-1\\); hence its rightmost column is \\(k-1\\).  \nBecause \\(R\\subseteq L\\), every row of \\(R\\) is strictly larger than its rightmost column, so the smallest row index in \\(R\\) is at least \\(k\\).  Yet the cell \\((i,i-1)\\) has row index \\(i<k\\), a contradiction.  \u220e\n\nSince \\(|T|=n-1\\), Lemma\u202f2 implies that any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n#### 5. Global lower bound  \n\nAny admissible tiling of the whole board consists of a family of rectangles covering \\(U\\) together with a family covering \\(L\\).  By the previous two lemmas the first family contains at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles and the second family at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  Hence every admissible tiling uses at least\n\\[\n2(n-1)\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n#### 6. Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the horizontal rectangle\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j):i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle; its row index \\(i\\) is smaller than every column it contains, so it avoids the diagonal.\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j-1):j\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(V_j\\) is an \\((n-j+1)\\times 1\\) rectangle; its column index \\(j-1\\) is smaller than every row it contains, so it also avoids the diagonal.\n\nThe family\n\\[\n\\mathcal{R}=\\{H_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\n*Disjointness.*  Each \\(H_i\\) lies entirely in row \\(i\\), while each \\(V_j\\) lies entirely in column \\(j-1\\).  A cell cannot belong simultaneously to a row\u2011only rectangle and a column\u2011only rectangle, because such a cell would have to satisfy simultaneously \\(i<j\\) (to belong to some \\(H_i\\)) and \\(i\\ge j\\) (to belong to some \\(V_j\\)), which is impossible.  Hence the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Coverage.*  If \\(i<j\\) then the cell \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(H_i\\); if \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(V_j\\).  Thus every off\u2011diagonal cell is covered exactly once, while no diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) belongs to any rectangle.  Consequently each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered cell, namely \\((i,i)\\).\n\nTherefore a tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles exists.\n\n#### 7. Minimality and final answer  \n\nThe lower bound of \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles and the explicit construction with \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles coincide; hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot 2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
